let __v_2830 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[\"Not cruel, no. Wanting to keep a pet with you is the ordinary way love behaves, and asking the question is what someone who is still looking after them does. What helps is a written week - did they eat, settle, sleep, enjoy one thing - because a change is easier to see over a fortnight than in one bad night.\",\"That is not selfish, and it is not a vote. Ask the vet what they would do with their own animal and ask them to be blunt about pain and appetite, then keep a note of the good days against the bad ones - most people find the answer is in that note rather than in the guilt.\",\"Asking it says most of what needs saying: you have not stopped looking after them. Whether keeping on is fair depends on the animal, not on you - eating, drinking, pain, one thing still enjoyed. Take that list to the vet and let them tell you what the next weeks look like, rather than working it out alone.\",\"No, not cruel. There is no scoreboard for this, and the person who is doing it for the animal's sake is usually the one who feels worst about it. The useful thing is the vet's honest view plus a normal week written down: food, drink, sleep, pain, one good thing a day.\"]}")?;
frame.store("KEEPING_GOING_REPLIES", __v_2830.clone())?;
let __v_2831 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[\"There is no single day, and waiting for a sign that everyone agrees on is what makes this the hardest part. What people use: more bad days than good, no interest in food or in the things they loved, pain the vet cannot get on top of. Ask the vet to go through a quality-of-life list with you - it turns a guess into something you can look at.\",\"Only you and the vet can say, and the vet has done this far more often than you have. Write down a normal day for the next week - eating, drinking, moving, sleeping, one thing they still enjoy - and take that to the appointment; a fortnight of it usually makes the call clearer than any single bad night.\",\"It is a judgement rather than a date, and the ones that go wrong are the ones made in a panic at 2am. Book a talk with the vet about quality of life and ask them plainly: would they wait, and what would make them stop waiting?\"]}")?;
